] "\n" [:option {:value "153033"} "Points Race (Course de Primes), Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "153032"} "25 kilometres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "925133"} "Sprint (Professionals [#2]), Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "925168"} "Sprint (Professionals), Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "925212"} "Tandem Sprint (Professionals), Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "925111"} "Team Sprint (Professionals [Course des Nations]), Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "925135"} "Points Race (Professionals), Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "925134"} "50 kilometres (Professionals), Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "925123"} "100 kilometres (Professionals), Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "925124"} "100 miles (Professionals), Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "925127"} "3,000 metres (Handicap (Professionals), Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "925126"} "25 kilometres Motor-paced (Professionals), Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "925155"} "National Team Sprint (Professionals [Grand Prix Régional]), Men"]] [:script {} "$('#same_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#same_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:form {:class "form-inline form-inline-header"} [:span {} "Not held in other editions"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/924"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "24-Hours Race (Professionals [Bol d'Or]), Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "15 – 16 September 1900"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic (non-medal)"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/194"} "Vélodrome Municipal de Vincennes, Paris"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/925125"} "12 from 6 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "This was considered the great race of the professional meeting, the Bol d’Or, or Golden Bowl, in which riders raced for 24 consecutive hours on the velodrome, with the rider covering the greatest distance winning. This event had been held in France since 1894 and was a paced race, and had been won in 1894-95 and 1898 by " [:a {:href "/athletes/890854"} "Constant Huret"] ", who was present in 1900 and likely favored, although Britain’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/891663"} "Albert Walters"] " had won the event in 1899 and was also present at the 1900 Paris Exposition."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Twelve riders started the race, which began at night, with distances recorded for the top eight. Huret, who had won the long-distance Bordeaux-Paris race in 1899, did not finish, but Walters was a major factor throughout. He led for 19 hours, and set 16 world records en route, but had to withdraw from exhaustion in the 20th hour. Dutch rider " [:a {:href "/athletes/890356"} "Matthieu Cordang"] " eventually won easily, by 62 km over German " [:a {:href "/athletes/891412"} "Thaddäus Robl"] ". The total purse was 25,000 francs, with Cordang receiving over 7,000 francs, which consisted of a purse for winning and for various primes."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The leaders after each hour, with distances covered, were as follows:"] "\n\n" [:p {} "1\tWalters\t55.525 km"] "\n\n" [:p {} "2\tWalters\t106.150 km"] "\n\n" [:p {} "3\tWalters\t151.560 km\tWR"] "\n\n" [:p {} "4\tWalters\t200.018 km \tWR"] "\n\n" [:p {} "5\tWalters\t248.455 km \tWR"] "\n\n" [:p {} "6\tWalters\t295.300 km \tWR"] "\n\n" [:p {} "7\tWalters\t338.210 km \tWR"] "\n\n" [:p {} "8\tWalters\t384.566 km \tWR"] "\n\n" [:p {} "9\tWalters\t428.520 km \tWR"] "\n\n" [:p {} "10\tWalters\t473.400 km \tWR"] "\n\n" [:p {} "11\tWalters\t517.440 km \tWR"] "\n\n" [:p {} "12\tWalters\t562.900 km \tWR"] "\n\n" [:p {} "13\tWalters\t600.050 km \tWR"] "\n\n" [:p {} "14\tWalters\t641.313 km \tWR"] "\n\n" [:p {} "15\tWalters\t682.455 km \tWR"] "\n\n" [:p {} "16\tWalters\t724.725 km \tWR"] "\n\n" [:p {} "17\tWalters\t759.800 km \tWR"] "\n\n" [:p {} "18\tWalters\t794.550 km \tWR"] "\n\n" [:p {} "19\tWalters\t827.285 km \tWR"] "\n\n" [:p {} "20\tWalters\t829.900 km"] "\n\n" [:p {} "21\tCordang\t851.900 km"] "\n\n" [:p {} "22\tCordang\t885.200 km"] "\n\n" [:p {} "23\tCordang\t921.400 km"] "\n\n" [:p {} "24\tCordang\t956.775 km"] "\n\n" [:p {} "Cordang’s mark of 956.775 km was well short of the record set by Walters, who recorded 1,020.977 km in 1899, but Walters’ mark was motor-paced (or electric tandem-paced). In 1900, the pacers were triplet bikes, as was true in 1898. The race had been tandem paced in 1894-97. The previous races had been held at other velodromes, not at Vincennes."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The Bol d’Or would not be held in 1901, but was held from 1902-13 continually, and then intermittently after the war. It was held in 1928 and then one more time in 1950. Huret returned to win again in 1902, but the greatest rider in the Bol d’Or would be Frenchman Léon Georget, who won the event nine times – in 1903, consecutively from 1907-13, and after the war in 1919."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Distance (km)"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/890356"} "Matthieu Cordang"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NED"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NED.png"}] "NED"]] [:td {} "956.775 km"] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/891412"} "Thaddäus Robl"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]] [:td {} "894.775 km"] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {} [:a {:class "footnote", :href "#", :title "KLWT have the distance as 864.775 but Vélo lists the distance as shown here. KLWT must have copied the distance from the OR which makes the same mistake. The distance is not possible, as long as Garin's distance of 890.275 in 3rd place is correct, because it would have changed the order of the fini